    "q-161075": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161075",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{3}{y},\\,y=x,\\,y=\\frac{x}{3}.\n$$\n\nA transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,\\bbox[5pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,,\\,\\frac{x}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?",
      "worked_solution": "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRe-writing the equations \n\n$$\n{e}^{x}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{3}{y},\\,y=x,\\,y=\\frac{x}{3}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}y=1,\\,{e}^{x}y=3,\\,\\frac{x}{y}=1,\\,\\frac{x}{y}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)={e}^{x}y$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y},$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,{e}^{x}y\\,,\\,\\frac{x}{y}).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
