    "q-103964": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103964",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Let $D$ be the finite region bounded by the curve \n$(x+{y}^{3})^{2}-y=1$ and the line $y=2.$ Consider the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=x+{y}^{3}$ and $v=y.$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{3} \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x+{y}^{3}=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+{y}^{3})^{2}-y=1\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=1\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\,⟹\\,v=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-1 \\\\ v=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ v=2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{3},2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14166956/assets/fc6e896f50255deea574c52014935097b6c0b49aa6a1e54a9d6a484dc1b8cc61.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,{u}^{2}-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-103964-a",
